1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,918
Right, Jason and Karin, now I asked you to look at the case study

2
00:00:04,918 --> 00:00:08,390
for Box Telecom as part of your exam assessment.

3
00:00:08,390 --> 00:00:12,628
It's interesting because they are in the middle of problems at the moment

4
00:00:12,628 --> 00:00:15,500
and I want you to track how they deal with them.

5
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:18,150
Let's start with you, Karin.

6
00:00:18,150 --> 00:00:22,987
Having read through the case study, can you just summarise what the problems were

7
00:00:22,987 --> 00:00:26,140
that Box Telecom had to take on board?

8
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:27,008
Um, yeah...

9
00:00:27,008 --> 00:00:33,087
Well of course what first came to their attention was that, despite a new

10
00:00:33,087 --> 00:00:39,166
advertising campaign, they were suffering from falling sales-and this is something that had many

11
00:00:39,166 --> 00:00:39,600
causes.

12
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:44,330
On top of that immediate problem, what had also happened over the last two

13
00:00:44,330 --> 00:00:49,061
years was that, although they had invested in an expansion plan, they had to

14
00:00:49,061 --> 00:00:50,750
face up to increased competition.

15
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:54,999
And, before they had a chance to get to grips with the effects of

16
00:00:54,999 --> 00:00:59,247
that, they were stalled by a strike and it was just when they were

17
00:00:59,247 --> 00:01:02,889
thinking about making a colossal investment in new machinery for their plants.

18
00:01:02,889 --> 00:01:04,710
So they were really in trouble.

19
00:01:04,710 --> 00:01:06,791
Yes, I think that's fair.

20
00:01:06,791 --> 00:01:10,121
And Jason, you contacted the company, didn't you?

21
00:01:10,121 --> 00:01:14,700
What did the company define as the reasons for these problems?

22
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:18,570
Well, I think they've hit on the right things-it would be easy to say

23
00:01:18,570 --> 00:01:19,952
they had invested too heavily,

24
00:01:19,952 --> 00:01:21,334
or at the wrong time,

25
00:01:21,334 --> 00:01:25,204
but in fact the signs were good and what they were set back by

26
00:01:25,204 --> 00:01:26,310
was high interest rates.

27
00:01:26,310 --> 00:01:28,758
At the same time, their longer-term problems,

28
00:01:28,758 --> 00:01:33,653
which were affecting their market share, were eventually credited to poor training-and having looked

29
00:01:33,653 --> 00:01:37,500
at the details in their last report I think that's right.

30
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:39,748
So, onto the larger issues then.

31
00:01:39,748 --> 00:01:43,120
Karin, what do you think the company will do?

32
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:43,634
Hmm...

33
00:01:43,634 --> 00:01:50,835
Well, obviously they have the choice of accepting the very favourable terms that another

34
00:01:50,835 --> 00:01:54,950
company-KMG Plc-have given them to buy them out.

35
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:58,930
That would mean creating a new company with a new image.

36
00:01:58,930 --> 00:02:03,996
Or they could decide on a bolder move and offer some new shares if

37
00:02:03,996 --> 00:02:04,720
they wanted.

38
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:09,363
But I think they're much more cautious than that and expect they will start

39
00:02:09,363 --> 00:02:14,005
trying to find individuals who'd be prepared to back them with some of the

40
00:02:14,005 --> 00:02:15,000
capital the need.

41
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,042
Well, you mustn't always assume that dramatic problems require dramatic solutions.

42
00:02:20,042 --> 00:02:26,000
Sometimes there's a simple fix such as changing the guy at the top.

43
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:31,390
If they truly are cautious, then I suspect they will seek to shut down

44
00:02:31,390 --> 00:02:32,930
some of their shops.

45
00:02:32,930 --> 00:02:35,012
But a more ambitious approach,

46
00:02:35,012 --> 00:02:40,840
and one which I think would have more chance of success, would be to

47
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:45,004
alter how they're running things-the management layers and the processes.

48
00:02:45,004 --> 00:02:49,584
So in your analysis try to think of all the options.

49
00:02:49,584 --> 00:02:50,000
Jason?

50
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,984
Yes, it's interesting because I've found it a really useful company to study.

51
00:02:54,984 --> 00:03:00,736
Its problems cross all types of industries and it's lucky it's so big - a smaller

52
00:03:00,736 --> 00:03:04,570
or even medium-sized company would have gone under by now.

53
00:03:04,570 --> 00:03:09,224
Ah well in fact, what I want you two to do is to go

54
00:03:09,224 --> 00:03:12,880
away when we've finished our discussion today and write a report.

55
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:19,078
We've looked in general at the telecommunications market in the UK over the last few sessions

56
00:03:19,078 --> 00:03:23,339
and I want you to take Box Telecom as an example

57
00:03:23,339 --> 00:03:27,600
and suggest some ways in which they might overcome their problems.

58
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:32,185
And outline the reasons why you think as you do-but try

59
00:03:32,185 --> 00:03:37,603
and keep it intrinsic to the company rather than dragging in other examples.

60
00:03:37,603 --> 00:03:39,270
Is that OK, Karin?

61
00:03:39,270 --> 00:03:41,959
Yes, I think I can do that.

62
00:03:41,959 --> 00:03:44,649
Personally I've got great hopes for it.

63
00:03:44,649 --> 00:03:46,570
I think it will recover.

64
00:03:46,570 --> 00:03:52,440
That advertising campaign they did was very strong and they're very innovative with their

65
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:54,117
products-they set new trends.

66
00:03:54,117 --> 00:03:57,890
The company's got to recover, don't you think, Jason?

67
00:03:57,890 --> 00:03:58,987
Hmmm-I'm not sure.

68
00:03:58,987 --> 00:04:04,106
I think it can but it's not a foregone conclusion unless they manage to

69
00:04:04,106 --> 00:04:06,300
attract the right level of investment.

70
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:11,116
The company definitely needs a boost and to attract more highly skilled workers if

71
00:04:11,116 --> 00:04:13,180
their recovery is to be long-lasting.

72
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:17,282
When I was talking to the marketing manager he said to me that he

73
00:04:17,282 --> 00:04:21,384
thinks the company had got a great management team-but he would say that, wouldn't

74
00:04:21,384 --> 00:04:21,677
he?

75
00:04:21,677 --> 00:04:25,193
-but they are suffering from having to work with outdated production machinery

76
00:04:25,193 --> 00:04:27,830
and that could cost a lot to put right.

77
00:04:27,830 --> 00:04:31,137
Well, personally I think the stock market is to blame.

78
00:04:31,137 --> 00:04:34,443
I think they were expecting too much of the company

79
00:04:34,443 --> 00:04:37,750
and then inevitably it looked bad when it didn't perform.

80
00:04:37,750 --> 00:04:40,855
The market should have had more realistic expectations.

81
00:04:40,855 --> 00:04:44,736
And I disagree with you about the advertising campaign Karin.

82
00:04:44,736 --> 00:04:49,005
That's where they could do with some innovation-to get sales kick-started.

83
00:04:49,005 --> 00:04:52,110
Anyway, let's see what you come up with...
